WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.330
SPEAKER: The 2021 State
of DevOps Report released

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:07.560
last year--

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:10.050
it contained findings
about how teams navigate

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:13.380
key areas of software delivery.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.840
We talked with two of the
researchers, Dustin Smith

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:20.160
and Dave Stanke, about the
report's section on security.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:21.420
Hi, Dustin and Dave.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:23.100
Were there any
surprising findings

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.395
in this section of the report?

00:00:24.395 --> 00:00:26.520
DUSTIN SMITH: You would
think that the more steps I

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.220
add into my process,
the slower it will be.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.920
But I think it's really
about how teams are

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:33.390
integrating these practices.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:36.510
And are they doing it by
looking at their entire system

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.250
and figuring out
how they can best

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:42.030
fit them into the actual
process so that it is seamless?

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:45.030
And that should be the idea
with any of these practices.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:48.510
And we find that teams that
implement these practices

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:52.410
are actually delivering software
faster and more reliability,

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:55.332
and all while seeing value
driven to the business.

00:00:55.332 --> 00:00:57.540
DAVE STANKE: So one thing
that we found consistently,

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.700
and yet I'm still
surprised by it every time,

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:03.390
is that we don't have to make
a compromise or a trade-off

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:06.870
between delivering fast
and delivering well.

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:10.890
We found that teams that have
the best secure code practices

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:12.570
actually are the
same ones that are

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:16.020
delivering the most frequently
and having the shortest

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.730
lead time from code to delivery.

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:25.170
So we really can have both
fast and market responsiveness

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:28.020
and security and reliability.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.990
SPEAKER: What exactly did the
security section of the report

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.218
focus on?

00:01:32.218 --> 00:01:34.260
DUSTIN SMITH: I think one
of the important things

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.410
to clarify with how
we looked at security

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:39.000
is that we weren't
looking at things

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.190
like how many vulnerabilities
did you experience

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:42.090
in the last year.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:45.870
We weren't measuring
how secure is your team.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.060
Instead, what we
focused on were what

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.730
practices have you
implemented on your team

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:55.800
in order to detect and recover
from security vulnerabilities

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:59.285
in an effective way.

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:00.660
That's one of the
key differences

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:05.920
that we try to focus
on for several reasons.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.400
One of the main ones
is that people may not

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.259
be willing to share how
many vulnerabilities

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:12.240
they've experienced.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.580
But they are willing to
share what practices they've

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:17.310
built into their process.

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:19.830
SPEAKER: What did you
find separated elite teams

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:21.480
from the rest?

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.190
DAVE STANKE: The real
fundamental thing

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:26.070
that we see is they do
something called shifting left

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:26.840
on security.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:28.590
The teams that are
succeeding are the ones

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:31.710
that are bringing security
principles, knowledge,

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:34.890
and practices leftward
into their testing,

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:37.830
into their initial
development processes,

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:42.540
and making it something
that is known and practiced

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:44.850
throughout the organization.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:47.910
They make a lot of use
of automated testing

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:51.090
to make it so that known
vulnerabilities can rapidly

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:54.540
be detected and kicked
back for remediation.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.120
And something that really
high-performing teams

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:01.870
like to do is having guardrails
or pre-approved libraries,

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:04.350
things that developers
can pull into their code

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:07.530
with the assurance that those
have been vetted by experts

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:10.530
and that they're going
to pass their security

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:13.830
tests because they've
already been built in.

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:16.380
The security practices
have already been built in.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:18.030
DUSTIN SMITH:
Elite-performing teams

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.580
are more likely
to build security

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:23.890
into their entire
development process.

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:26.910
And so that means not throwing
over your most recent change

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.910
to the security team and
asking for approval right

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:32.520
before deployment and hoping
that they do it quickly.

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:35.010
It means pulling
them in earlier,

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:36.720
right at the design doc phase.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:38.940
Are you including
your security team?

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.160
And then are you
including security reviews

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.840
throughout your entire
process as well?

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.830
And then do you have tests that
check for known vulnerabilities

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:48.840
built into your build process?

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.810
And finally, do you have
pre-approved libraries

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:53.460
that you can pull
into your project

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:57.690
and know that those versions
of those dependencies

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:03.300
are pre-approved and secure
and ready to be used?

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:07.140
SPEAKER: How can teams start
implementing these practices?

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:08.880
DUSTIN SMITH: I'd
say go to the report

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:11.520
and look at all the
practices that we've outlined

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.060
and try to identify
some of those quick wins

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:15.480
that you could get.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.167
Where are the low-hanging fruit?

00:04:17.167 --> 00:04:18.959
And one of the nice
things about the report

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:22.230
is that we also include industry
benchmarks for each practice.

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:23.940
So you can get a
rough idea of to what

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:28.440
degree has this practice already
been adopted in the industry.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.300
That should give
you an idea, even

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:32.880
if you haven't met with
your team yet, of where,

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:35.247
potentially, are we lacking.

00:04:35.247 --> 00:04:37.830
SPEAKER: What would you say is
the core idea from this section

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:39.450
of the report?

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:43.170
DAVE STANKE: Really, the meme
is security is everybody's job.

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:47.175
But that doesn't mean
that it's not your job.

00:04:47.175 --> 00:04:49.350
There can be an
unfortunate interpretation

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:51.360
that says, well, if
it's everybody's job,

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:52.170
then I'm covered.

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:54.120
I don't need to worry about it.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:57.370
Well, the bad news is
it's your job, too.

00:04:57.370 --> 00:04:59.160
And we find that
teams that succeed

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:01.290
are the ones where everyone
in the organization

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:04.770
really internalizes the
importance of shipping

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:06.090
secure code.

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:07.980
Now, that doesn't
mean that we expect

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:09.990
every person in an
organization to be

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:16.140
a security expert and a
worldwide leader in software

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:16.830
security.

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:19.710
Instead, what we like to
see is a consultative model,

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:23.700
where an organization has a
team of real security experts

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:26.550
who are creating
guidelines, creating

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:29.820
pre-approved pipelines,
and generally educating

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:32.520
the organization about how
to incorporate security

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:33.243
into their work.

00:05:33.243 --> 00:05:35.160
And then everyone
throughout the organization,

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:38.970
from design to implementation
to testing to operations,

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:42.030
all can reflect that
knowledge back into the work

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:45.750
so that it's incorporated
in their day-to-day jobs.

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:49.170
DUSTIN SMITH: I think the
overarching principle should

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:53.070
be that we need to move from
being reactive to detective

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:56.700
vulnerabilities and more
proactive and even diagnostic

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:59.580
and just noting
that vulnerabilities

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:01.990
are, essentially, inevitable.

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:03.540
And so we need to
be able to come up

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:08.400
with practices and processes
in order to recover from them

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:15.550
when they are detected in a
way that is quick and scalable.

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:17.815
SPEAKER: Where can
users go to learn more?

00:06:17.815 --> 00:06:20.800
DAVE STANKE: We've got a great
resource on our Google Cloud

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:24.040
site that incorporates this
year's State of DevOps Report

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:25.420
as well as previous years'.

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:26.982
It's got a capability
catalog that

00:06:26.982 --> 00:06:28.690
shows all those
capabilities I mentioned.

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:30.190
And for each one,
there's an article

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:33.400
that talks about what it is, how
to measure it, and how to get

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:35.470
started practicing it.

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:37.232
There's all that
and more, including

00:06:37.232 --> 00:06:39.190
a self-assessment tool
that I really recommend.

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:42.430
You can check it all
out at g.co/devops.

